+2. Work the chase list. Purpose-built MCP docs are written for the
+   vendor's own first-party clients: they reliably carry the identity
+   facts (endpoint, transport, auth model, scopes) and reliably omit the
+   entitlement facts. After reading the north star, explicitly check for
+   three things it commonly drops, and go to the sub-flow doc when it
+   does: (a) the IAM or role grant the connection requires, (b) the
+   admin-side enable/allow toggle, (c) the exact credential-dialog field
+   labels. Absence of these from the north star is not evidence they are
+   unnecessary.
+3. Never read a reference index whole. Release-note streams, IAM and
+   access-control references, OAuth scope inventories, and `llms.txt`
+   indexes are reference indexes, not procedure docs. Fetch the specific
+   section, or search within the page for the one fact you need, record
+   that fact, and move on. Do not cite a reference index as a
+   documentation property that was "swept" — that is not evidence of
+   coverage.
